        fish: 'Seafood: suppliers are Russian fishing companies holding quotas; the Federal Agency for Fishery publishes them when quotas are allocated. Buyers are importers, wholesalers and processors near the offices of arrival: live product usually goes to Hokkaido, frozen product to the large ports (map below).',
        wood: 'Timber: the Companies sheet lists only mills with JAS certificates of Japan’s agriculture ministry (glulam, plywood); there is no open Russian register of exporters with tax IDs — LesEGAIS, the Russian timber register, has been closed to outside users since 2025 (timber accounting moved to FGIS LK, which requires an e-signature login), and Cerberus does not cover timber. Buyers are timber traders and house-kit makers in the regions of the ports of arrival.',
        cars: 'Almost all car exports to Russia are used cars (visible at HS9 level). Exporters are Japanese companies buying at auctions, usually near the ports of shipment (map). Buyers are dealers and private individuals, mostly in the Russian Far East.',
        pharma: 'Medicines: the Russian State Register of Medicines lists the registration holders of Japanese drugs. Public procurement shows the end customers, hospitals, by country of origin “Japan”.',
        medical: 'Medical and measuring instruments: the Roszdravnadzor register of medical devices lists the manufacturer and its authorised representative in Russia. Public procurement shows the end customers.',
